00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:13.950
about Anthos, and more
specifically GKE On-Prem.

00:00:13.950 --> 00:00:16.320
So quick intros-- my
name is Western Hutchins.

00:00:16.320 --> 00:00:18.240
I am a product manager
for GKE On-Prem,

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.085
and I brought some fine
folks with me today.

00:00:21.085 --> 00:00:22.968
Do you want to
introduce yourselves?

00:00:22.968 --> 00:00:23.760
KYLE BASSETT: Sure.

00:00:23.760 --> 00:00:26.680
Kyle Bassett, partner at Arctiq.

00:00:26.680 --> 00:00:29.470
Been working on Anthos
project for a while

00:00:29.470 --> 00:00:31.190
closely with Wes since
last year at Next.

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:32.857
So excited to show
you some of the stuff

00:00:32.857 --> 00:00:37.250
we've been working on, and with
Dan and his team at Optiva.

00:00:37.250 --> 00:00:38.570
DAN DYER: I'm Dan Dyer.

00:00:38.570 --> 00:00:40.867
I work in product
management at Optiva,

00:00:40.867 --> 00:00:42.450
and we've been working
with these guys

00:00:42.450 --> 00:00:45.013
on getting our application
to work on GKE On-Prem.

00:00:45.013 --> 00:00:45.930
WESTON HUTCHINS: Cool.

00:00:45.930 --> 00:00:49.620
So a lot of the session is going
to be focused on a real world

00:00:49.620 --> 00:00:50.600
rollout.

00:00:50.600 --> 00:00:53.800
So Kyle from Arctiq went into
Optiva, took an app they had,

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:54.662
and deployed it.

00:00:54.662 --> 00:00:56.370
And we're going to
cover some of the tips

00:00:56.370 --> 00:00:59.345
and tricks and the technical
deep dives of what exactly

00:00:59.345 --> 00:01:00.870
GKE On-Prem is.

00:01:00.870 --> 00:01:03.120
But before that, I
want to just talk

00:01:03.120 --> 00:01:06.400
a little bit about what
problems we're trying to solve,

00:01:06.400 --> 00:01:09.230
what the vision for
Anthos is as we move on.

00:01:09.230 --> 00:01:13.350
And this really started
from a lot of customers

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:15.990
that we were talking
to in GKE, like folks

00:01:15.990 --> 00:01:17.510
that were already in cloud.

00:01:17.510 --> 00:01:20.100
And they said, we're doing
our modernization right now.

00:01:20.100 --> 00:01:21.300
We have all these apps.

00:01:21.300 --> 00:01:23.550
We're trying to move them
from VMs over to containers,

00:01:23.550 --> 00:01:25.925
and we're bought into a subset
that can run on the cloud,

00:01:25.925 --> 00:01:28.740
but there's so many
other scenarios out there

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:31.440
that we need to run in
physical data centers

00:01:31.440 --> 00:01:32.970
or in edge locations.

00:01:32.970 --> 00:01:35.340
So a common scenario
that I've heard

00:01:35.340 --> 00:01:37.350
is like, oh, we want a
cluster in every store,

00:01:37.350 --> 00:01:39.150
or we want a cluster
at every oil site,

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:43.800
or we need to have this workload
running in a particular region

00:01:43.800 --> 00:01:46.800
where we can't necessarily
move everything into the cloud.

00:01:46.800 --> 00:01:49.410
And the message from
that was really-- we

00:01:49.410 --> 00:01:52.860
want to use Kubernetes,
because we've

00:01:52.860 --> 00:01:54.630
heard about all
of these benefits

00:01:54.630 --> 00:01:57.570
around portability
and abstraction.

00:01:57.570 --> 00:01:59.280
Kubernetes has really
become the standard

00:01:59.280 --> 00:02:01.990
for trying to make these
environments consistent.

00:02:01.990 --> 00:02:05.790
But if you've ever tried to take
an off the shelf distribution

00:02:05.790 --> 00:02:08.460
and get it up and
running yourself,

00:02:08.460 --> 00:02:09.639
it just takes forever.

00:02:09.639 --> 00:02:11.730
And I think getting it
installed is the easy part.

00:02:11.730 --> 00:02:13.800
What is really challenging
for a lot of customers

00:02:13.800 --> 00:02:15.360
is all the day two operations.

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:19.050
So having scaled out
masters, making sure

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:20.670
that you can do
security patches,

00:02:20.670 --> 00:02:22.333
doing disaster
recovery and backups,

00:02:22.333 --> 00:02:24.750
and these are all the features
that we gave out of the box

00:02:24.750 --> 00:02:26.190
with GKE.

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:28.140
So these customers
were like, well,

00:02:28.140 --> 00:02:29.995
let's see if we can
bring GKE On-Prem

00:02:29.995 --> 00:02:31.620
and build this package
software that we

00:02:31.620 --> 00:02:32.910
can deploy in these sites.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:34.440
And that was sort
of the beginning

00:02:34.440 --> 00:02:37.200
of our journey for Anthos.

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:40.580
But the thing I want
you guys to leave with--

00:02:40.580 --> 00:02:42.810
this is really much more
than just Kubernetes.

00:02:42.810 --> 00:02:45.150
We use Kubernetes as that
base abstraction layer

00:02:45.150 --> 00:02:46.620
to have a portable environment.

00:02:46.620 --> 00:02:49.320
But as Kelsey says,
this is really

00:02:49.320 --> 00:02:51.930
the start of what
will hopefully be

00:02:51.930 --> 00:02:55.290
a bunch of higher level tools
that can run on this platform.

00:02:55.290 --> 00:02:58.850
And I like to describe the
building blocks of Anthos

00:02:58.850 --> 00:03:03.010
as primarily three
open-source technologies--

00:03:03.010 --> 00:03:05.380
Kubernetes for the
infrastructure pieces,

00:03:05.380 --> 00:03:08.190
Istio for the
security management,

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.080
service management
stuff, and then finally

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:13.800
at the top, Knative cloud-run
type of environments where

00:03:13.800 --> 00:03:15.988
you can have that
developer to an experience

00:03:15.988 --> 00:03:18.530
so your devs don't have to work
directly with like Kubernetes

00:03:18.530 --> 00:03:21.700
YAML or go into the details
of configuring Istio traffic

00:03:21.700 --> 00:03:22.530
splitting.

00:03:22.530 --> 00:03:26.490
They can just work with
higher level constructs.

00:03:26.490 --> 00:03:28.080
And the thing that
we've really done

00:03:28.080 --> 00:03:31.440
to build out Anthos
and figure out

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:33.650
how this thing is
going to run is

00:03:33.650 --> 00:03:36.770
we want to take the
deployment and have it

00:03:36.770 --> 00:03:38.110
running these different sites.

00:03:38.110 --> 00:03:40.280
But the real power,
and what I like

00:03:40.280 --> 00:03:42.380
to call sort of the
brain, that powers

00:03:42.380 --> 00:03:45.890
all of these different locations
of Kubernetes, Istio, Knative,

00:03:45.890 --> 00:03:49.620
and these other pieces, is this
Anthos hybrid control plane.

00:03:49.620 --> 00:03:52.190
This is the stuff that runs
on Google Cloud Platform.

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:55.702
And the vision here is that we
have a single pane of glass,

00:03:55.702 --> 00:03:57.410
we have a single hosted
service, that you

00:03:57.410 --> 00:04:00.710
can use to programmatically
control clusters running

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:01.910
in these different sites.

00:04:01.910 --> 00:04:04.050
And as we get into
the demo later on,

00:04:04.050 --> 00:04:07.190
you'll get to see how we've
done this between GKE and GKE

00:04:07.190 --> 00:04:08.930
On-Prem, where you
can start to use

00:04:08.930 --> 00:04:11.780
the same tools, the same
workflows all in the cloud,

00:04:11.780 --> 00:04:16.329
and have that go and federate
out to the various locations.

00:04:16.329 --> 00:04:18.380
The real personas, the
real people that we really

00:04:18.380 --> 00:04:21.380
want to try and target with
this is-- in a big enterprise

00:04:21.380 --> 00:04:24.230
organization, which I
imagine many of you are in,

00:04:24.230 --> 00:04:26.450
there's all these
different specialty roles.

00:04:26.450 --> 00:04:30.530
And the key value prop
of Anthos is really

00:04:30.530 --> 00:04:32.630
making sure that we have
the right building blocks

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:35.110
and the right tools
for each one of them.

00:04:35.110 --> 00:04:37.880
We've sort of already talked
about the Kubernetes layer,

00:04:37.880 --> 00:04:40.130
where we can take the
things for GKE On-Prem,

00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:42.170
put them on your
existing hardware running

00:04:42.170 --> 00:04:45.360
on top of VMware, and
keep those consistent.

00:04:45.360 --> 00:04:47.990
The Istio pieces or the
cloud service mesh pieces

00:04:47.990 --> 00:04:50.670
are really there for your
NetOp, your SecOps people.

00:04:50.670 --> 00:04:53.120
Those are the ones that want
increased observability,

00:04:53.120 --> 00:04:55.370
better secure connections
in between services,

00:04:55.370 --> 00:04:58.160
and more control over what's
going on in your environment.

00:04:58.160 --> 00:05:00.020
And then the
developer experience--

00:05:00.020 --> 00:05:03.440
so we want to make sure to have
a nice, easy set of building

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:05.805
blocks for having a platform
as a service type features

00:05:05.805 --> 00:05:08.180
so that your devs can just
push code and have it deployed

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:10.990
to the various clusters.

00:05:10.990 --> 00:05:13.880
Now Anthos, like I said,
is a much bigger effort.

00:05:13.880 --> 00:05:16.220
What I want to talk
about specifically

00:05:16.220 --> 00:05:19.670
in this presentation
is GKE On-Prem.

00:05:19.670 --> 00:05:23.120
So this is the footprint where
we take GKE and we install it

00:05:23.120 --> 00:05:24.842
on top of your stack.

00:05:24.842 --> 00:05:26.300
And before we get
into the details,

00:05:26.300 --> 00:05:28.850
I want Dan to introduce
Optiva a little bit

00:05:28.850 --> 00:05:31.160
and give you an
idea of what his use

00:05:31.160 --> 00:05:33.560
case was for this
particular proof of concept.

00:05:37.215 --> 00:05:38.840
DAN DYER: So I'm a
bit challenged here.

00:05:38.840 --> 00:05:41.570
I have to do two things at
once, two different hands.

00:05:41.570 --> 00:05:42.270
So I'm Dan Dyer.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:44.720
Like I said, I run product
management at Optiva.

00:05:44.720 --> 00:05:47.660
We are a business
support company

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:50.150
that runs charging systems
for some of the larger

00:05:50.150 --> 00:05:51.960
telecoms in the world.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:54.770
We have over 100
customers deployed,

00:05:54.770 --> 00:06:00.655
80 countries, do three
billion-plus transactions.

00:06:00.655 --> 00:06:02.030
The main product
that we're going

00:06:02.030 --> 00:06:05.190
to talk about today, what
we did that prototype with,

00:06:05.190 --> 00:06:08.370
the proof of concept, is what we
called Optima Charging Engine.

00:06:08.370 --> 00:06:10.290
It's a tier one charging engine.

00:06:10.290 --> 00:06:13.770
That means that this is
built for the biggest telecom

00:06:13.770 --> 00:06:15.450
companies in the world.

00:06:15.450 --> 00:06:18.060
It does primarily
rating and charging.

00:06:18.060 --> 00:06:20.250
It's got quite a
bit of functionality

00:06:20.250 --> 00:06:22.830
in terms of how
you customize it,

00:06:22.830 --> 00:06:25.380
how you can build
it specifically

00:06:25.380 --> 00:06:28.673
for your environment,
what it integrates with.

00:06:28.673 --> 00:06:30.090
But the big things
here you should

00:06:30.090 --> 00:06:33.210
think about is that this is
mission-critical application

00:06:33.210 --> 00:06:34.470
for telecom companies.

00:06:34.470 --> 00:06:36.360
If this doesn't work, their
billing system doesn't work

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:37.610
and they don't make any money.

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:41.280
So pretty important from their
perspective that it scales,

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:44.100
that it runs at a high
level of reliability,

00:06:44.100 --> 00:06:47.580
and that it can
operate efficiently.

00:06:47.580 --> 00:06:50.740
So this is kind of
the overall stack.

00:06:50.740 --> 00:06:53.230
Essentially, if you look at
the dark blue piece there,

00:06:53.230 --> 00:06:55.412
that's the piece that
we're going to focus on.

00:06:55.412 --> 00:06:56.620
It's part of a bigger system.

00:06:56.620 --> 00:07:01.090
So it has to fit into
their entire environment.

00:07:01.090 --> 00:07:02.860
One of the things
that a lot of people

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:04.930
don't quite internalize
as they start

00:07:04.930 --> 00:07:07.060
to try to take an
enterprise application

00:07:07.060 --> 00:07:10.150
and move it into more of a
cloud-native or Kubernetes

00:07:10.150 --> 00:07:14.200
environment is that it's
not just take your app,

00:07:14.200 --> 00:07:16.630
break it up maybe a
little bit, maybe not,

00:07:16.630 --> 00:07:18.550
shove it over into
something like a VM

00:07:18.550 --> 00:07:20.560
like you would traditionally,
and then run it.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:22.060
There's a decent
amount of work that

00:07:22.060 --> 00:07:25.395
needs to be done here in terms
of modifying your application.

00:07:25.395 --> 00:07:26.770
So it's really
important that you

00:07:26.770 --> 00:07:30.400
think about how you're going to
containerize and take advantage

00:07:30.400 --> 00:07:33.910
of some of the specific
cloud-native applications

00:07:33.910 --> 00:07:36.342
and technologies out there.

00:07:36.342 --> 00:07:38.050
It's a good opportunity
to actually spend

00:07:38.050 --> 00:07:42.148
some time looking at how
you can simplify your app.

00:07:42.148 --> 00:07:44.440
It's also important to think
about some of the benefits

00:07:44.440 --> 00:07:45.610
that you're trying
to get out of this

00:07:45.610 --> 00:07:48.370
and invest in those, such as
the scaling and the automated

00:07:48.370 --> 00:07:51.220
deployment and update.

00:07:51.220 --> 00:07:55.110
So if you look at the
diagram on the left there,

00:07:55.110 --> 00:07:58.290
that's your typical
virtualization model.

00:07:58.290 --> 00:08:03.013
As we moved into a cloud-native,
Kubernetes-based deployment,

00:08:03.013 --> 00:08:05.430
some of the things that we
really focused on, like I said,

00:08:05.430 --> 00:08:06.960
first of all, is
you've really got

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:09.260
to think about how
you're going to change

00:08:09.260 --> 00:08:12.000
the architecture
of the application

00:08:12.000 --> 00:08:14.760
to take advantage of a lot
of cloud-native patterns.

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:16.470
That's critical both
from the perspective

00:08:16.470 --> 00:08:18.270
of how your application
is going to behave,

00:08:18.270 --> 00:08:20.670
but also how you're going
to be able to efficiently

00:08:20.670 --> 00:08:24.690
operate and deploy it.

00:08:24.690 --> 00:08:28.250
A lot of legacy applications
are using a bunch

00:08:28.250 --> 00:08:29.375
of proprietary technology.

00:08:29.375 --> 00:08:32.000
This is also an opportunity for
you to go out and start looking

00:08:32.000 --> 00:08:34.220
at using open-source
tools like--

00:08:34.220 --> 00:08:36.110
typically, like, for
example, we moved

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:38.419
a lot of our proprietary
monitoring tools

00:08:38.419 --> 00:08:40.370
over to the ELK Stack,
and then leveraging

00:08:40.370 --> 00:08:42.453
some things like Stackdriver
and things like that.

00:08:44.860 --> 00:08:46.960
Key to the delivery
of this is our ability

00:08:46.960 --> 00:08:49.360
to actually improve
our scalability

00:08:49.360 --> 00:08:51.050
and our operational efficiency.

00:08:51.050 --> 00:08:54.340
So if all we did was take this
thing and move it to the cloud

00:08:54.340 --> 00:08:55.987
or move it to a
private cloud and we

00:08:55.987 --> 00:08:57.820
didn't get a whole lot
of benefit out of it,

00:08:57.820 --> 00:09:00.700
that was an interesting
exercise, but why did we do it?

00:09:00.700 --> 00:09:03.160
So it's really critical that
you think about what you're

00:09:03.160 --> 00:09:05.050
trying to achieve out of this--

00:09:05.050 --> 00:09:11.500
what are your goals in terms
of your end SLA, your end

00:09:11.500 --> 00:09:13.840
operational efficiency,
whether you're

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:17.868
trying to achieve high
reliability or a lower TCO.

00:09:17.868 --> 00:09:20.410
Those are all things that you
need to think about in advance,

00:09:20.410 --> 00:09:24.580
and we spent quite a bit
of time looking at that.

00:09:24.580 --> 00:09:28.370
In any enterprise environment,
but especially in telecom,

00:09:28.370 --> 00:09:30.110
it's relatively conservative.

00:09:30.110 --> 00:09:33.110
People are not willing to bet
the farm on some new technology

00:09:33.110 --> 00:09:33.987
right away.

00:09:33.987 --> 00:09:35.570
So it's important
that you give people

00:09:35.570 --> 00:09:40.010
an incremental step
through their options

00:09:40.010 --> 00:09:42.170
so that they can
actually feel like they

00:09:42.170 --> 00:09:45.170
can take the jumps as they
need to, as they're ready to,

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:47.700
and that their business
is able to adapt.

00:09:47.700 --> 00:09:51.710
So on a typical
legacy environment,

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:54.680
you're sitting there with
a pretty rigid environment.

00:09:54.680 --> 00:09:57.050
You have to
pre-provision everything.

00:09:57.050 --> 00:09:59.390
There's a high cost.

00:09:59.390 --> 00:10:03.770
As we move into the Kubernetes
On-Prem kind of scenario,

00:10:03.770 --> 00:10:07.760
we get a much better flexibility
in terms of our deployment

00:10:07.760 --> 00:10:08.750
options.

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:13.610
You can leverage bare metal
and a variety of private clouds

00:10:13.610 --> 00:10:14.840
to do that.

00:10:14.840 --> 00:10:17.175
The next step really when
you think about moving this,

00:10:17.175 --> 00:10:18.800
if you're going to
do it incrementally,

00:10:18.800 --> 00:10:21.530
is to start to split
out some of your systems

00:10:21.530 --> 00:10:23.630
and run that in a
hybrid scenario.

00:10:23.630 --> 00:10:27.483
And then, finally, if you're
trying to make this journey,

00:10:27.483 --> 00:10:29.150
you move all the way
to the public cloud

00:10:29.150 --> 00:10:33.900
and everything's running on a
managed service in the cloud.

00:10:33.900 --> 00:10:35.920
And that is where
from our perspective

00:10:35.920 --> 00:10:40.380
you get the most TCO benefit,
you get the most efficiency,

00:10:40.380 --> 00:10:42.460
you're taking advantage
of all the reasons

00:10:42.460 --> 00:10:46.110
why Google Cloud exists.

00:10:46.110 --> 00:10:50.400
The end state, the
state of nirvana

00:10:50.400 --> 00:10:53.490
if you want to call it in
terms of from the provider

00:10:53.490 --> 00:10:57.000
side of things, is that
we can deploy pretty much

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.490
the same application with
small amounts of differences

00:11:00.490 --> 00:11:02.640
whether it's in the public
cloud or private cloud.

00:11:02.640 --> 00:11:04.690
That allows us to be
much more efficient.

00:11:04.690 --> 00:11:07.680
It also allows us to
run the same code.

00:11:07.680 --> 00:11:10.170
It allows people
to migrate easily.

00:11:10.170 --> 00:11:12.240
The hybrid story or
the public cloud story

00:11:12.240 --> 00:11:14.410
becomes much better
when you do that.

00:11:14.410 --> 00:11:15.840
And so, that's
really the promise

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:19.230
that we see with GKE
On-Prem is that if you look

00:11:19.230 --> 00:11:21.472
at this diagram, beside
some of our storage

00:11:21.472 --> 00:11:23.430
and database managed
services that we're trying

00:11:23.430 --> 00:11:26.040
to use in the cloud, we
can pretty much end up

00:11:26.040 --> 00:11:27.267
with the same application.

00:11:27.267 --> 00:11:29.100
And as you'll see with
the proof of concept,

00:11:29.100 --> 00:11:30.142
that's where we ended up.

00:11:32.904 --> 00:11:34.360
WESTON HUTCHINS: Cool.

00:11:34.360 --> 00:11:36.420
So before I hand
it over to Kyle,

00:11:36.420 --> 00:11:38.940
I want to give at
least some context.

00:11:38.940 --> 00:11:41.550
Like, how does GKE On-Prem
work from an architecture

00:11:41.550 --> 00:11:42.910
perspective?

00:11:42.910 --> 00:11:45.120
So you get the bits
for GKE On-Prem,

00:11:45.120 --> 00:11:48.300
and how this comes packaged
is you get an admin OVA

00:11:48.300 --> 00:11:49.570
workstation.

00:11:49.570 --> 00:11:51.660
This is just a tool
you can spin up

00:11:51.660 --> 00:11:54.180
on a VM in your
VMware environment

00:11:54.180 --> 00:11:57.280
that you can use to bootstrap
the rest of your cluster.

00:11:57.280 --> 00:11:59.580
So the first thing you
do is you run prepare.

00:11:59.580 --> 00:12:02.460
This runs some
pre-validations checks.

00:12:02.460 --> 00:12:05.015
We've added support for
downloading container images

00:12:05.015 --> 00:12:06.390
to things like
private registries

00:12:06.390 --> 00:12:09.240
for folks that have offline
and air gapped installs.

00:12:09.240 --> 00:12:12.780
We've also added support during
the install process for things

00:12:12.780 --> 00:12:14.730
like static IP
allocation for folks

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:17.760
that don't have DeepCP
setup in those environments.

00:12:17.760 --> 00:12:19.890
But the end state
is that you end up

00:12:19.890 --> 00:12:23.730
with this single long live node
called admin control plane,

00:12:23.730 --> 00:12:25.920
and this is really the
orchestrator for all

00:12:25.920 --> 00:12:27.585
of the On-Prem clusters.

00:12:27.585 --> 00:12:30.210
All of the commands that you're
going to be using interact with

00:12:30.210 --> 00:12:32.850
this admin control
plane, and for instance,

00:12:32.850 --> 00:12:34.350
if you want to
create new clusters--

00:12:34.350 --> 00:12:37.440
we've called them user
clusters in this example--

00:12:37.440 --> 00:12:39.840
you go in and run a
create cluster command,

00:12:39.840 --> 00:12:42.070
and go and spin up these
different environments.

00:12:42.070 --> 00:12:45.710
So right now we do have a
separate utility from G Cloud.

00:12:45.710 --> 00:12:48.350
Over time I think you'll
start to see G Cloud, our CLI,

00:12:48.350 --> 00:12:49.950
merge with some
of these commands

00:12:49.950 --> 00:12:54.010
so that we can invoke these
from a single environment.

00:12:54.010 --> 00:12:56.640
But for now, we've just built
a nice lightweight CLI package

00:12:56.640 --> 00:12:59.610
in order to spin up
these new clusters.

00:12:59.610 --> 00:13:02.580
Once you have all of
your clusters spun up,

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:05.430
or just even a single
cluster, the next step

00:13:05.430 --> 00:13:07.853
is registering this cluster
with our Cloud Console.

00:13:07.853 --> 00:13:09.270
And this is the
piece where we can

00:13:09.270 --> 00:13:11.145
start to do more of the
single pane of glass,

00:13:11.145 --> 00:13:14.400
and also the remote
management of GKE On-Prem.

00:13:14.400 --> 00:13:17.550
So you walk through a simple
flow in our Cloud Console.

00:13:17.550 --> 00:13:21.270
The things it'll ask you for
is doing some metadata exchange

00:13:21.270 --> 00:13:25.050
and then some credentials
to access that cluster.

00:13:25.050 --> 00:13:29.790
After you go and connect, one of
the pieces that gets downloaded

00:13:29.790 --> 00:13:32.280
is this lightweight
connection agent.

00:13:32.280 --> 00:13:34.290
This thing sits next
to your API server

00:13:34.290 --> 00:13:36.320
and allows us to go
from the Cloud Console

00:13:36.320 --> 00:13:41.015
and reach into that API server
and issue a kubectl command.

00:13:41.015 --> 00:13:42.390
One of the nice
things about this

00:13:42.390 --> 00:13:45.450
is that every
authentication flow for this

00:13:45.450 --> 00:13:47.400
happens as the end
user credential.

00:13:47.400 --> 00:13:49.050
So if your developers
want to use this,

00:13:49.050 --> 00:13:51.030
they will only see
things that they

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:53.728
are authenticated to see even
for these On-Prem clusters.

00:13:53.728 --> 00:13:55.770
So if there are certain
namespaces that they only

00:13:55.770 --> 00:13:59.220
have visibility to,
only those name faces

00:13:59.220 --> 00:14:02.160
will show up for them
inside the console.

00:14:02.160 --> 00:14:04.240
Now the number one
question we get

00:14:04.240 --> 00:14:06.310
is around this external
connectivity like what

00:14:06.310 --> 00:14:09.370
is this-- how does it work?

00:14:09.370 --> 00:14:11.740
We wanted something that was
nice, quick, and easy out

00:14:11.740 --> 00:14:12.970
of the box experience.

00:14:12.970 --> 00:14:18.970
So the GKE connect agent is
really a lightweight gRPC proxy

00:14:18.970 --> 00:14:21.340
that we can use to
access these API servers

00:14:21.340 --> 00:14:23.560
and running in these
various environments.

00:14:23.560 --> 00:14:25.630
No VPN is needed, no
direct interconnect

00:14:25.630 --> 00:14:28.150
to get these things set up, and
you have a secure connection

00:14:28.150 --> 00:14:29.500
to the control plane.

00:14:29.500 --> 00:14:31.370
This really is just
for control plane data.

00:14:31.370 --> 00:14:34.430
So this is not like replacing
a fat pipe of monitoring,

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:35.680
telemetry, and all that stuff.

00:14:35.680 --> 00:14:38.625
They have their own data, data
pipeline, data path things.

00:14:38.625 --> 00:14:40.000
But this really
gives the ability

00:14:40.000 --> 00:14:42.280
to have that single
pane of glass experience

00:14:42.280 --> 00:14:44.082
for all of the
different environments.

00:14:44.082 --> 00:14:46.540
We're going to talk a lot more
about networking and storage

00:14:46.540 --> 00:14:48.832
a bit later, but we wanted
to do it as part of the demo

00:14:48.832 --> 00:14:51.500
so you could actually see
it working in real time.

00:14:51.500 --> 00:14:53.190
So with that, Kyle, over to you.

00:14:56.450 --> 00:14:57.450
KYLE BASSETT: All right.

00:14:57.450 --> 00:15:01.530
So we decided we're going to
give a pretty in-depth demo.

00:15:01.530 --> 00:15:03.420
The original plan was
to do a live demo,

00:15:03.420 --> 00:15:04.670
but there's a lot to cover.

00:15:04.670 --> 00:15:06.750
So I thought it'd be better
if we covered it all,

00:15:06.750 --> 00:15:08.833
and we're going to have
more of an open discussion

00:15:08.833 --> 00:15:11.850
and give you some opinions
on what we put together.

00:15:11.850 --> 00:15:14.440
The Optiva app is
a pretty cool app.

00:15:14.440 --> 00:15:17.000
They've done a good job
making it cloud native,

00:15:17.000 --> 00:15:19.470
and it runs in GKE today.

00:15:19.470 --> 00:15:22.590
So much of the exercise of what
we wanted to do as far as goals

00:15:22.590 --> 00:15:25.200
was develop a lot of
knowledge around it

00:15:25.200 --> 00:15:27.510
and prove that we could
port this application

00:15:27.510 --> 00:15:30.990
in a short period of
time over to GKE On-Prem.

00:15:30.990 --> 00:15:32.560
And also just get
something working,

00:15:32.560 --> 00:15:34.740
and then start to scale
test it, and start

00:15:34.740 --> 00:15:36.970
to do all the resiliency
things that we wanted to do.

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:40.440
So we're pretty pleasantly
surprised with how quick

00:15:40.440 --> 00:15:42.870
we got it up, and
working with Dan's teams,

00:15:42.870 --> 00:15:44.290
we got through it
pretty quickly.

00:15:44.290 --> 00:15:45.750
So we'll dive into the details.

00:15:45.750 --> 00:15:47.950
And I know a lot of people
haven't seen it at all.

00:15:47.950 --> 00:15:50.760
So we're going to try to
show what it looks like.

00:15:50.760 --> 00:15:52.120
We did this in five weeks.

00:15:52.120 --> 00:15:54.690
So we had a pretty
aggressive timeline.

00:15:54.690 --> 00:15:58.770
That is everything from getting
the servers that we used racked

00:15:58.770 --> 00:16:02.880
and stacked, and getting ESX
on them, deploying all the base

00:16:02.880 --> 00:16:04.460
components.

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:07.750
Optiva leverages Cloud Build
and GCR in the cloud today.

00:16:07.750 --> 00:16:09.750
So we want to be able
to leverage that process

00:16:09.750 --> 00:16:13.200
and that CI process and pull the
images down locally, and then

00:16:13.200 --> 00:16:15.307
be able to deploy them.

00:16:15.307 --> 00:16:16.890
Everything was done
in Helm templates.

00:16:16.890 --> 00:16:20.370
So that made it a lot easier for
us to deploy and get things up,

00:16:20.370 --> 00:16:22.440
but we also needed things
like persistent storage

00:16:22.440 --> 00:16:25.800
that we're going to get into
and talk about those aspects.

00:16:25.800 --> 00:16:28.638
And week four was really
about-- let's start

00:16:28.638 --> 00:16:30.180
to throw some load
at this, and let's

00:16:30.180 --> 00:16:32.640
see if the
scalability components

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:34.290
that Optiva has in
their application--

00:16:34.290 --> 00:16:35.820
can we scale pods?

00:16:35.820 --> 00:16:38.490
Can we scale nodes
on the back end?

00:16:38.490 --> 00:16:40.515
We really beat up on
the cluster pretty good.

00:16:40.515 --> 00:16:42.390
So we had to get another
node added this week

00:16:42.390 --> 00:16:44.870
to be able to actually test
some of the scaling components.

00:16:44.870 --> 00:16:47.310
And then we wrapped
up with documentation

00:16:47.310 --> 00:16:51.720
and making sure everybody
knew what was going on.

00:16:51.720 --> 00:16:54.240
The slide's not really painting
properly for some reason,

00:16:54.240 --> 00:16:56.430
but it was a pretty
cool project.

00:16:56.430 --> 00:16:57.780
That is a map of the world.

00:16:57.780 --> 00:17:00.270
[LAUGHTER]

00:17:00.270 --> 00:17:02.190
Just picture it.

00:17:02.190 --> 00:17:04.380
And we were 100% remote.

00:17:04.380 --> 00:17:06.510
None of us have met in person.

00:17:06.510 --> 00:17:08.760
Optiva has a very distributed
team of experts around

00:17:08.760 --> 00:17:10.680
that are very skilled.

00:17:10.680 --> 00:17:14.369
So we did this all over
Slack, Google Hangouts,

00:17:14.369 --> 00:17:16.444
collaboration tools.

00:17:16.444 --> 00:17:17.819
So it just goes
to show you don't

00:17:17.819 --> 00:17:20.224
need the card pass to
go into a data center

00:17:20.224 --> 00:17:21.599
to build a private
cloud anymore.

00:17:21.599 --> 00:17:24.730
You can do it
completely remotely.

00:17:24.730 --> 00:17:26.849
So that was a
pretty cool project

00:17:26.849 --> 00:17:29.760
to see it be
completely distributed.

00:17:29.760 --> 00:17:33.850
So I guess we're going
to dive over to the demo?

00:17:33.850 --> 00:17:35.370
WESTON HUTCHINS: Yeah.

00:17:35.370 --> 00:17:37.662
One of the things I wanted
to say on the previous slide

00:17:37.662 --> 00:17:38.330
is just--

00:17:38.330 --> 00:17:40.770
we packaged some stuff out
of the box with GKE On-Prem.

00:17:40.770 --> 00:17:42.150
You get things like monitoring.

00:17:42.150 --> 00:17:44.970
You get OIDC connections
through identity providers.

00:17:44.970 --> 00:17:49.770
But Optiva had a lot of
existing tools and technologies,

00:17:49.770 --> 00:17:52.440
like Sysdig, Harbor, and
vaults, and stuff like that.

00:17:52.440 --> 00:17:54.593
As part of that, because
like Kyle went in there

00:17:54.593 --> 00:17:56.010
and plugged those
into the system,

00:17:56.010 --> 00:17:57.220
which we'll show right now.

00:17:57.220 --> 00:17:59.430
So you can take
your existing stack

00:17:59.430 --> 00:18:01.818
and integrate those
into GKE On-Prem.

00:18:01.818 --> 00:18:02.610
KYLE BASSETT: Yeah.

00:18:02.610 --> 00:18:04.650
I think the main point
with this is really

00:18:04.650 --> 00:18:07.592
it was just another region for
us to deploy to in the end.

00:18:07.592 --> 00:18:10.050
We were able to use a lot of
the same tools and techniques,

00:18:10.050 --> 00:18:13.120
a few changes obviously around
storage and networking, which

00:18:13.120 --> 00:18:14.120
we're going to get into.

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:16.680
But it really just looks like
another deployment target

00:18:16.680 --> 00:18:18.450
for Optiva today,
which is really--

00:18:18.450 --> 00:18:19.770
as Dan talked about--

00:18:19.770 --> 00:18:22.600
was really the ultimate goal.

00:18:22.600 --> 00:18:25.280
So we'll flip over to the demo.

00:18:25.280 --> 00:18:27.780
So we're going to go through a
bunch of different components

00:18:27.780 --> 00:18:31.230
here, and I think we'll have
a few comments to say around

00:18:31.230 --> 00:18:32.310
them.

00:18:32.310 --> 00:18:34.080
We're going to start
at what this looks

00:18:34.080 --> 00:18:35.800
like at the very least of it.

00:18:35.800 --> 00:18:38.110
So I've created some
aliases in this demo.

00:18:38.110 --> 00:18:40.320
So you're going to
see A is your kubectl,

00:18:40.320 --> 00:18:43.020
but it's pointing at
the admin cluster,

00:18:43.020 --> 00:18:45.960
so the part Wes talked about
that manages the environment

00:18:45.960 --> 00:18:48.150
and can bootstrap
additional clusters.

00:18:48.150 --> 00:18:49.620
U is the user cluster.

00:18:49.620 --> 00:18:51.660
User cluster is where
we put all the Optiva

00:18:51.660 --> 00:18:53.440
applications, and
all the pods, and all

00:18:53.440 --> 00:18:54.690
the supporting infrastructure.

00:18:54.690 --> 00:18:56.190
You're going to see
some namespaces.

00:18:56.190 --> 00:18:59.700
So if we look at
the admin cluster--

00:18:59.700 --> 00:19:02.130
so you recognize these
type of namespaces

00:19:02.130 --> 00:19:04.300
in a regular Kubernetes cluster.

00:19:04.300 --> 00:19:07.590
If we look at the user cluster,
you see things like GKE Connect

00:19:07.590 --> 00:19:08.610
that Wes talked about.

00:19:08.610 --> 00:19:09.540
You see Harbor.

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:12.600
You see Istio.

00:19:12.600 --> 00:19:16.200
And the OCE pieces are
the Optiva application.

00:19:16.200 --> 00:19:19.680
So that's where we put all of
the pods that went into there.

00:19:19.680 --> 00:19:23.430
If you look at nodes, the
admin cluster is fairly small.

00:19:23.430 --> 00:19:26.220
Multi-master support's
coming in the release

00:19:26.220 --> 00:19:28.170
that's dropping very soon.

00:19:28.170 --> 00:19:30.930
And then if you look
at our bottom cluster,

00:19:30.930 --> 00:19:32.140
we have 11 nodes.

00:19:32.140 --> 00:19:34.740
So essentially these
are 11 worker nodes

00:19:34.740 --> 00:19:37.050
that are able to process load.

00:19:37.050 --> 00:19:39.520
And we've done some scaling
up and scaling down.

00:19:39.520 --> 00:19:42.890
That's why you see one of them
is a little bit shorter time.

00:19:42.890 --> 00:19:47.370
We got this demo put
together yesterday.

00:19:47.370 --> 00:19:49.910
So you're probably wondering
what it looks like in vCenter.

00:19:49.910 --> 00:19:52.953
It looks just like any of your
other vCenter environments.

00:19:52.953 --> 00:19:54.620
There's nothing really
special about it.

00:19:54.620 --> 00:19:56.480
We started with a clean cluster.

00:19:56.480 --> 00:20:00.140
We obviously need to be
able to call the API.

00:20:00.140 --> 00:20:04.910
GKE On-Prem has fully
orchestrated all the VM

00:20:04.910 --> 00:20:05.660
provisioning.

00:20:05.660 --> 00:20:08.540
You're really not having to
go and clobber around vCenter

00:20:08.540 --> 00:20:09.680
at all after you set it up.

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:11.750
So once you provision
user cluster,

00:20:11.750 --> 00:20:13.460
this piece is all automated.

00:20:13.460 --> 00:20:15.530
We're leveraging data
stores on the back end.

00:20:15.530 --> 00:20:19.152
We have a few different
data stores, SSD, HDD.

00:20:19.152 --> 00:20:21.110
We're also leveraging
persistent storage, which

00:20:21.110 --> 00:20:22.360
we'll talk about a little bit.

00:20:22.360 --> 00:20:24.260
But as far as this
goes, it looks

00:20:24.260 --> 00:20:26.360
a lot like any other cluster.

00:20:26.360 --> 00:20:28.070
We're leveraging
a template to be

00:20:28.070 --> 00:20:32.008
able to spin up the base node
before it gets bootstrapped.

00:20:32.008 --> 00:20:33.800
We've got a bunch of
different data stores.

00:20:33.800 --> 00:20:35.550
We've got some different
virtual switches.

00:20:35.550 --> 00:20:38.510
There's a lot of flexibility
on how that stuff's configured,

00:20:38.510 --> 00:20:40.700
and we've just put everything
in a resource group

00:20:40.700 --> 00:20:41.850
to keep it separate.

00:20:41.850 --> 00:20:44.450
So no reason why you
couldn't use a shared cluster

00:20:44.450 --> 00:20:47.030
that you already have to
get this up and running.

00:20:47.030 --> 00:20:48.860
Clearly we need to be
able to call the API

00:20:48.860 --> 00:20:52.590
and orchestrate a lot
of these capabilities.

00:20:52.590 --> 00:20:54.080
Another piece
that's part of this

00:20:54.080 --> 00:20:58.760
is we are using an F5 virtual
appliance to do all the ingress

00:20:58.760 --> 00:21:01.010
pieces, and that's
also orchestrated.

00:21:01.010 --> 00:21:03.530
So if you do carve
out an ingress inside

00:21:03.530 --> 00:21:08.480
of the environment, it will use
the container connector piece

00:21:08.480 --> 00:21:10.850
that F5 provides
to carve out these.

00:21:10.850 --> 00:21:12.260
We're going to
show a demo of how

00:21:12.260 --> 00:21:15.420
we're able to do that
dynamically near the end.

00:21:15.420 --> 00:21:18.320
So it's able to orchestrate
all the front end.

00:21:18.320 --> 00:21:20.210
I know, Wes, there's
always lots of questions

00:21:20.210 --> 00:21:22.867
around ingress networking
and some stuff that's coming.

00:21:22.867 --> 00:21:24.950
Do you want to comment on
some of the stuff that's

00:21:24.950 --> 00:21:25.700
coming down the line?

00:21:25.700 --> 00:21:25.910
WESTON HUTCHINS: Yeah.

00:21:25.910 --> 00:21:27.650
So one of the common
questions we get

00:21:27.650 --> 00:21:30.930
is, what's the dependency on F5?

00:21:30.930 --> 00:21:33.465
So we really wanted that
out of box experience

00:21:33.465 --> 00:21:36.650
where you could use Kubernetes
to provision load balancer

00:21:36.650 --> 00:21:38.250
support right out of the box.

00:21:38.250 --> 00:21:40.340
But we have heard from
customers that they already

00:21:40.340 --> 00:21:43.760
run things like HAProxy
or Nginx instead of F5.

00:21:43.760 --> 00:21:46.593
So one of the things we are
going to be adding pretty soon

00:21:46.593 --> 00:21:49.010
is going to be the ability to
bring your own load balancer

00:21:49.010 --> 00:21:50.060
and plug into that.

00:21:50.060 --> 00:21:52.130
I think longer-term
you'll start to see maybe

00:21:52.130 --> 00:21:54.320
a Google provided load
balancer out of the box.

00:21:54.320 --> 00:21:57.200
But we're kind of listening
to the customer feedback

00:21:57.200 --> 00:21:59.180
around some of these
hard dependencies,

00:21:59.180 --> 00:22:01.705
or I have different
environments already in there.

00:22:01.705 --> 00:22:03.080
KYLE BASSETT: So
you can see here

00:22:03.080 --> 00:22:04.747
that we are looking
at the user cluster.

00:22:04.747 --> 00:22:06.890
If you look in the
top right partition

00:22:06.890 --> 00:22:08.330
we also have the admin cluster.

00:22:08.330 --> 00:22:10.430
As we extend more
clusters, you'll

00:22:10.430 --> 00:22:11.735
get more partitions obviously.

00:22:11.735 --> 00:22:12.860
So these are the base ones.

00:22:12.860 --> 00:22:14.810
You can see there's
an Istio space.

00:22:14.810 --> 00:22:17.300
We've got a virtual
server list that's

00:22:17.300 --> 00:22:21.787
been populated through when
we're plumbing these pieces.

00:22:21.787 --> 00:22:23.870
We've been playing around
with container connector

00:22:23.870 --> 00:22:25.220
a little bit.

00:22:25.220 --> 00:22:29.090
We can put F5 in the SDN range
and skip the whole node port

00:22:29.090 --> 00:22:29.590
piece.

00:22:29.590 --> 00:22:30.860
So that's something
we're looking

00:22:30.860 --> 00:22:33.020
at doing, and then introducing
other load balancers

00:22:33.020 --> 00:22:34.095
and things like that.

00:22:34.095 --> 00:22:34.720
DAN DYER: Yeah.

00:22:34.720 --> 00:22:37.880
I think one other thing from
the application provider

00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:39.750
perspective we're coming from--

00:22:39.750 --> 00:22:42.830
it's critical that
we don't want to be

00:22:42.830 --> 00:22:44.540
in the entire
infrastructure business.

00:22:44.540 --> 00:22:46.990
We want to be able to go in
and fit into the environment

00:22:46.990 --> 00:22:47.990
that the customer is in.

00:22:47.990 --> 00:22:50.180
And so the ability
to take advantage

00:22:50.180 --> 00:22:52.220
of a private cloud
like this and put

00:22:52.220 --> 00:22:56.715
some standard implementation
on top of it like GKE,

00:22:56.715 --> 00:22:58.340
and then us being
able to just leverage

00:22:58.340 --> 00:23:00.090
that and go in and
take advantage of that,

00:23:00.090 --> 00:23:01.790
and take advantage
of the infrastructure

00:23:01.790 --> 00:23:03.207
that the customer's
already built,

00:23:03.207 --> 00:23:05.460
to me, that's a
critical value add here

00:23:05.460 --> 00:23:09.100
that this is shown to
be pretty successful at.

00:23:09.100 --> 00:23:12.470
KYLE BASSETT: OK, so Wes
talked about the connector

00:23:12.470 --> 00:23:13.790
and where you manage this from.

00:23:13.790 --> 00:23:16.790
So not everybody's
a Kubernetes expert.

00:23:16.790 --> 00:23:20.490
Not everybody wants to be
running kubectl commands.

00:23:20.490 --> 00:23:25.160
So we can leverage Google Cloud
Platform's admin station or web

00:23:25.160 --> 00:23:27.020
UI to be able to look
at all our clusters

00:23:27.020 --> 00:23:28.820
and start to see what's
going on with them and diving

00:23:28.820 --> 00:23:29.600
into the detail.

00:23:29.600 --> 00:23:32.120
So I showed you
earlier the node list.

00:23:32.120 --> 00:23:33.987
This is the exact same
view through here,

00:23:33.987 --> 00:23:36.320
and you'll be able to see you
get quite a bit of insight

00:23:36.320 --> 00:23:37.860
into what's going on into here.

00:23:37.860 --> 00:23:39.860
We're going to show some
Stackdriver integration

00:23:39.860 --> 00:23:41.040
and stuff like that.

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:44.030
But if you look at our
cluster, here's our cluster,

00:23:44.030 --> 00:23:46.812
there's no reason why we
couldn't have other regions

00:23:46.812 --> 00:23:48.020
that are cloud-based in here.

00:23:48.020 --> 00:23:51.410
The whole idea is it's a common
experience across your on-prem

00:23:51.410 --> 00:23:54.565
and your cluster
inside the cloud.

00:23:54.565 --> 00:23:55.190
DAN DYER: Yeah.

00:23:55.190 --> 00:23:58.060
That really is critical
to that hybrid strategy

00:23:58.060 --> 00:24:00.200
we're talking about
that if you can

00:24:00.200 --> 00:24:04.160
have the same point of control
and management visibility

00:24:04.160 --> 00:24:06.230
to both your on-prem
and the stuff

00:24:06.230 --> 00:24:07.940
you start to move
into the cloud,

00:24:07.940 --> 00:24:10.770
then it just becomes much
easier to make that move.

00:24:10.770 --> 00:24:10.970
WESTON HUTCHINS: Yeah.

00:24:10.970 --> 00:24:12.470
One of the things
that I hear a lot,

00:24:12.470 --> 00:24:16.160
and I heard it from Dan too,
is these enterprise customers

00:24:16.160 --> 00:24:18.800
who are trying to train their
IT staff and their developers

00:24:18.800 --> 00:24:20.042
to think more like the cloud.

00:24:20.042 --> 00:24:21.500
Well, this is that
bridge where you

00:24:21.500 --> 00:24:23.083
can start to use a
lot of the tooling,

00:24:23.083 --> 00:24:25.808
but you can still
operate it on-prem.

00:24:25.808 --> 00:24:26.600
KYLE BASSETT: Yeah.

00:24:26.600 --> 00:24:28.180
So we'll look through
the-- obviously

00:24:28.180 --> 00:24:30.263
there's going to be more
components added to this,

00:24:30.263 --> 00:24:31.640
but we can look at all our pods.

00:24:31.640 --> 00:24:36.140
So usually you're trying
to kubectl, describe pod,

00:24:36.140 --> 00:24:37.200
get the pod name.

00:24:37.200 --> 00:24:40.280
You can really drill down
through it all through here,

00:24:40.280 --> 00:24:42.390
and you can see what
storage it's using.

00:24:42.390 --> 00:24:43.460
You can see any events.

00:24:43.460 --> 00:24:46.240
You can also look at
the YAML configuration.

00:24:46.240 --> 00:24:48.710
So this is a big piece of I
think what people are looking

00:24:48.710 --> 00:24:52.040
for as far as a managed
solution, is having

00:24:52.040 --> 00:24:54.680
the toolkit to be able
to manage upgrades

00:24:54.680 --> 00:24:56.660
and manage the lifecycle
of my clusters,

00:24:56.660 --> 00:24:59.960
be able to provision
pieces through there.

00:24:59.960 --> 00:25:03.250
We're working pretty hard
to build a lot of the CI/CD

00:25:03.250 --> 00:25:05.960
toolkit into this
workflow and be

00:25:05.960 --> 00:25:07.790
able to use a lot
of Google toolkits

00:25:07.790 --> 00:25:09.740
that we were comfortable
with using in cloud,

00:25:09.740 --> 00:25:11.720
and leverage them
on-prem as well.

00:25:11.720 --> 00:25:13.160
So I mentioned storage.

00:25:13.160 --> 00:25:14.930
Here's all the
[? PBs. ?] Everyone

00:25:14.930 --> 00:25:20.510
says applications that move to
Kubernetes don't need state.

00:25:20.510 --> 00:25:22.580
We always find out they
need state about an hour

00:25:22.580 --> 00:25:23.420
into the discussion.

00:25:23.420 --> 00:25:25.253
So there's quite a few
[? PB ?] requirements

00:25:25.253 --> 00:25:26.300
in this environment.

00:25:26.300 --> 00:25:27.620
We'll dive into that.

00:25:27.620 --> 00:25:30.800
You can see all the config
maps and configuration.

00:25:30.800 --> 00:25:32.263
Secrets are obviously protected.

00:25:32.263 --> 00:25:33.680
You need an elevated
account to be

00:25:33.680 --> 00:25:37.470
able to go in and look
at any of that stuff.

00:25:37.470 --> 00:25:40.390
Anything you want to say about
just the experience, Wes,

00:25:40.390 --> 00:25:42.940
around managing this
stuff and leveraging

00:25:42.940 --> 00:25:44.240
some of the other tools?

00:25:44.240 --> 00:25:45.157
WESTON HUTCHINS: Yeah.

00:25:45.157 --> 00:25:49.420
So I think the long-term vision
for this is that all of those

00:25:49.420 --> 00:25:52.210
cloud services that you were
mentioning-- so Cloud Build,

00:25:52.210 --> 00:25:56.680
cloud-hosted
Spinnaker deployments,

00:25:56.680 --> 00:25:58.805
doing policy management
inside the cloud, as well--

00:25:58.805 --> 00:26:00.180
these are the
pieces that we want

00:26:00.180 --> 00:26:02.080
to have run in the
cloud environment,

00:26:02.080 --> 00:26:04.300
be able to access
these clusters.

00:26:04.300 --> 00:26:07.117
And so for the most part,
we want this to feel--

00:26:07.117 --> 00:26:08.200
I think Kyle said it best.

00:26:08.200 --> 00:26:11.290
He's like, this is an
extension of your region.

00:26:11.290 --> 00:26:13.580
It's just running in
a different location.

00:26:13.580 --> 00:26:16.540
I think long-term, let's say
over the next couple of years,

00:26:16.540 --> 00:26:18.670
we'll start to see more
things where we have,

00:26:18.670 --> 00:26:21.820
let's say, global load balancers
that can maybe route traffic

00:26:21.820 --> 00:26:23.590
intelligently through
clusters running

00:26:23.590 --> 00:26:24.870
in these various environments.

00:26:24.870 --> 00:26:27.190
So there's a really
cool long-term story

00:26:27.190 --> 00:26:28.780
about how we can
have this brain that

00:26:28.780 --> 00:26:31.105
can federate out to these
different environments.

00:26:31.105 --> 00:26:32.290
KYLE BASSETT: Great.

00:26:32.290 --> 00:26:33.790
So Stackdriver is integrated.

00:26:33.790 --> 00:26:39.550
We can get metrics and logs into
GCP right from the clusters.

00:26:39.550 --> 00:26:41.720
Wes mentioned some of the
other toolkit we deployed.

00:26:41.720 --> 00:26:43.210
So we deployed
Sysdig really early.

00:26:43.210 --> 00:26:45.260
We knew that we needed
to test this at scale.

00:26:45.260 --> 00:26:47.290
And we wanted to get a
really good baseline.

00:26:47.290 --> 00:26:50.500
So it was a good way to start
from nothing, with no load,

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:52.090
and then ramp the
load up and really

00:26:52.090 --> 00:26:55.300
get into the network stack
and see what was going on.

00:26:55.300 --> 00:26:57.350
So we've created
some base dashboards.

00:26:57.350 --> 00:26:59.450
Now, the nice part is,
we've got a good baseline.

00:26:59.450 --> 00:27:01.075
We can really start
ramping up the load

00:27:01.075 --> 00:27:03.520
till this thing starts to
fall down, and really learn

00:27:03.520 --> 00:27:05.600
where we need to scale it.

00:27:05.600 --> 00:27:07.310
We can dive right
into the network view.

00:27:07.310 --> 00:27:09.143
Really, we're looking
at every single system

00:27:09.143 --> 00:27:10.960
call across all microservices.

00:27:10.960 --> 00:27:12.970
And we can drill down to
see the length of time

00:27:12.970 --> 00:27:14.530
and what's, exactly,
happening in it.

00:27:14.530 --> 00:27:17.350
So getting these
dashboards in place really

00:27:17.350 --> 00:27:19.815
sets us up for future success
as we start to tune this.

00:27:19.815 --> 00:27:20.440
DAN DYER: Yeah.

00:27:20.440 --> 00:27:21.850
And one of the
things that you'll

00:27:21.850 --> 00:27:25.870
find as you start to try to move
a legacy app to be cloud native

00:27:25.870 --> 00:27:26.433
is that--

00:27:26.433 --> 00:27:27.850
so you're going
to decompose that.

00:27:27.850 --> 00:27:29.683
You're going to break
it into little pieces.

00:27:29.683 --> 00:27:31.580
And all of the learnings
that you had before

00:27:31.580 --> 00:27:33.580
are still relevant, but
you're going to find out

00:27:33.580 --> 00:27:34.870
that you have new bottlenecks.

00:27:34.870 --> 00:27:38.320
Or maybe things aren't
quite connecting the way

00:27:38.320 --> 00:27:39.672
you used to think they were.

00:27:39.672 --> 00:27:41.380
And so having these
kind of tools in here

00:27:41.380 --> 00:27:43.638
to be able to go down and
drill down and find out

00:27:43.638 --> 00:27:46.180
where your problems are, where
your new bottlenecks are, what

00:27:46.180 --> 00:27:47.680
kind of performance
you're getting--

00:27:47.680 --> 00:27:49.692
that's critical to the
success of projects.

00:27:49.692 --> 00:27:51.400
And this really helped
us out, because we

00:27:51.400 --> 00:27:53.710
did run into a few issues
where we were either

00:27:53.710 --> 00:27:56.377
under-provisioned or had
made some bad choices

00:27:56.377 --> 00:27:57.460
in terms of configuration.

00:27:57.460 --> 00:27:59.890
And we were easily able to
come back and figure out

00:27:59.890 --> 00:28:02.275
what the problem was and
address it pretty easily.

00:28:02.275 --> 00:28:03.067
KYLE BASSETT: Yeah.

00:28:03.067 --> 00:28:05.733
So it's just deployed as a
daemon set in all the nodes.

00:28:05.733 --> 00:28:07.400
We'll probably wait
for questions, but--

00:28:07.400 --> 00:28:07.875
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE].

00:28:07.875 --> 00:28:08.890
I didn't catch what
you said there.

00:28:08.890 --> 00:28:10.240
What was the tool you used?

00:28:10.240 --> 00:28:11.650
KYLE BASSETT: Oh, it's Sysdig.

00:28:11.650 --> 00:28:15.490
So Sysdig is a SaaS tool,
monitoring, analytics tool.

00:28:15.490 --> 00:28:17.710
It's really focused
on containers.

00:28:17.710 --> 00:28:21.200
So it's capturing every
system call, essentially.

00:28:21.200 --> 00:28:23.920
And it's got a whole
analytics engine.

00:28:23.920 --> 00:28:25.220
The nice part is the dashboard.

00:28:25.220 --> 00:28:27.702
So being able to graph data
and look at it over time

00:28:27.702 --> 00:28:30.160
and saying, why did it get
slow, and drilling down-- that's

00:28:30.160 --> 00:28:31.285
what we really used it for.

00:28:31.285 --> 00:28:34.350
So helped us aggregate all
those logs and things like that.

00:28:34.350 --> 00:28:35.326
AUDIENCE: Thank you.

00:28:35.326 --> 00:28:36.790
KYLE BASSETT: No problem.

00:28:36.790 --> 00:28:38.380
So I mentioned Harbor.

00:28:38.380 --> 00:28:39.815
So Harbor's a CNCF project.

00:28:39.815 --> 00:28:41.270
We're a CNCF member.

00:28:41.270 --> 00:28:44.580
So we wanted to avoid having
to pull container images

00:28:44.580 --> 00:28:47.130
down every time we
built them over the pipe

00:28:47.130 --> 00:28:49.260
that Optiva has up to GCP.

00:28:49.260 --> 00:28:52.260
So we deployed
Harbor as a container

00:28:52.260 --> 00:28:54.180
to look after the
registry components.

00:28:54.180 --> 00:28:55.560
So we have a sync job.

00:28:55.560 --> 00:28:58.230
When their build process runs,
we can pull the containers down

00:28:58.230 --> 00:28:59.180
to the local LAN.

00:28:59.180 --> 00:29:00.930
And then when we're
building and deploying

00:29:00.930 --> 00:29:02.597
the applications via
the home templates,

00:29:02.597 --> 00:29:04.960
we're just pulling
off the local network.

00:29:04.960 --> 00:29:07.140
So Harbor was a nice
little registry to use.

00:29:07.140 --> 00:29:09.090
Really easy to get
up and running.

00:29:09.090 --> 00:29:11.430
No reason why you couldn't
leverage Artifactory

00:29:11.430 --> 00:29:13.930
or some enterprise registry
that you're already using today.

00:29:13.930 --> 00:29:16.210
This is just the way we
solved it for this project.

00:29:16.210 --> 00:29:16.835
DAN DYER: Yeah.

00:29:16.835 --> 00:29:19.230
And this is another
enterprise-specific thing.

00:29:19.230 --> 00:29:21.810
If you're going to try
to take this approach

00:29:21.810 --> 00:29:24.090
to do this with
your application,

00:29:24.090 --> 00:29:27.210
obviously, there's a
high sensitivity to churn

00:29:27.210 --> 00:29:29.813
and being able to
prequalify things

00:29:29.813 --> 00:29:30.980
in the customer environment.

00:29:30.980 --> 00:29:34.680
So having the ability to
stage like this and then

00:29:34.680 --> 00:29:38.675
try things out in parallel--
that's a huge strength of this

00:29:38.675 --> 00:29:40.050
that you wouldn't
necessarily get

00:29:40.050 --> 00:29:42.425
in a traditional environment
where you'd have to build up

00:29:42.425 --> 00:29:43.998
an entire parallel system.

00:29:43.998 --> 00:29:44.790
KYLE BASSETT: Yeah.

00:29:44.790 --> 00:29:48.480
So I think there's
upwards of 60 or 80 images

00:29:48.480 --> 00:29:50.790
that we're leveraging in this.

00:29:50.790 --> 00:29:52.780
So everybody is always
interested in storage.

00:29:52.780 --> 00:29:56.160
So we thought we'd cover a
little bit on the storage side.

00:29:56.160 --> 00:29:58.470
Natively, the default
storage provider for GKE

00:29:58.470 --> 00:30:01.810
today is a vSphere
cloud provider.

00:30:01.810 --> 00:30:03.210
So essentially
what that does is,

00:30:03.210 --> 00:30:06.570
allows you to
dynamically provision

00:30:06.570 --> 00:30:09.670
block-based volumes
via the platform.

00:30:09.670 --> 00:30:11.580
So what it's doing
is dynamically

00:30:11.580 --> 00:30:13.590
carving out a VMDK
on the back end

00:30:13.590 --> 00:30:16.650
and attaching it to the host and
then providing that access back

00:30:16.650 --> 00:30:17.400
to the container.

00:30:17.400 --> 00:30:19.410
So we leveraged
this for anything

00:30:19.410 --> 00:30:21.700
that needed persistent storage.

00:30:21.700 --> 00:30:24.600
There is an NFS
provider that has RWX.

00:30:24.600 --> 00:30:26.250
We didn't have time
to test that yet.

00:30:26.250 --> 00:30:28.590
It's something we're
definitely looking at doing.

00:30:28.590 --> 00:30:31.560
Other pieces that we're looking
to test as we move forward

00:30:31.560 --> 00:30:33.720
are more cloud-native
storage options.

00:30:33.720 --> 00:30:38.140
So we did some testing of Rook,
which is another CNCF project.

00:30:38.140 --> 00:30:41.130
But we're looking at things
like Robin and Portworx.

00:30:41.130 --> 00:30:43.250
A lot of customers
are leveraging

00:30:43.250 --> 00:30:45.445
back-end NetApp
appliance, but they

00:30:45.445 --> 00:30:47.320
have a Trident plug-in
that you can leverage.

00:30:47.320 --> 00:30:49.980
So storage is
what's nice about it

00:30:49.980 --> 00:30:51.720
if you don't know
Kubernetes very well--

00:30:51.720 --> 00:30:52.920
it's very, very plugable.

00:30:52.920 --> 00:30:54.300
All the components are plugable.

00:30:54.300 --> 00:30:56.345
You could run multiple
storage options here.

00:30:56.345 --> 00:30:57.720
And the way they
work-- and we'll

00:30:57.720 --> 00:30:59.850
show you in a little bit-- is,
it's just a default storage

00:30:59.850 --> 00:31:00.350
class.

00:31:00.350 --> 00:31:02.110
Or you have different
storage classes.

00:31:02.110 --> 00:31:06.340
You may have a storage class for
SSD, a storage class for HDD.

00:31:06.340 --> 00:31:08.193
You can put policies
and rules and quotas

00:31:08.193 --> 00:31:09.110
and stuff around that.

00:31:11.800 --> 00:31:14.020
So I'll show you how easy it is.

00:31:14.020 --> 00:31:16.270
Anybody, again, if you're
not familiar with Kubernetes

00:31:16.270 --> 00:31:18.650
and you've been asking for
volumes in a VM world--

00:31:18.650 --> 00:31:21.920
change record, wait, go
buy the guy a coffee--

00:31:21.920 --> 00:31:24.620
the idea behind all this stuff
is, it's all very declarative.

00:31:24.620 --> 00:31:26.840
So all you have to do
to carve a storage out

00:31:26.840 --> 00:31:30.560
in this environment is this
simple YAML configuration.

00:31:30.560 --> 00:31:34.560
I run that, I immediately
get a PV created.

00:31:34.560 --> 00:31:37.640
And then in a couple minutes,
I'll end up getting that ready.

00:31:37.640 --> 00:31:40.950
So now we've got a 2 gig volume
ready to go for me to leverage

00:31:40.950 --> 00:31:43.390
in my application.

00:31:43.390 --> 00:31:45.350
Literally takes
a couple seconds.

00:31:45.350 --> 00:31:47.780
What you'll see
now is, using govc,

00:31:47.780 --> 00:31:49.100
we're calling the vSphere API.

00:31:49.100 --> 00:31:52.452
You can see it's carved out the
VMDKs on the back end for us.

00:31:52.452 --> 00:31:54.410
And that's just leveraging
a regular data store

00:31:54.410 --> 00:31:56.720
that you would leverage in
your VMware environment.

00:31:56.720 --> 00:31:57.830
Nothing special.

00:31:57.830 --> 00:31:59.360
Just need the plugin.

00:31:59.360 --> 00:32:01.940
So we'll just go ahead
and delete the PV.

00:32:01.940 --> 00:32:03.140
You can see the PV's gone.

00:32:03.140 --> 00:32:06.330
We'll call vCenter again.

00:32:06.330 --> 00:32:08.020
And there's nothing left.

00:32:08.020 --> 00:32:10.730
So that's just a
look at storage.

00:32:10.730 --> 00:32:14.010
Storage is something
that, like I said,

00:32:14.010 --> 00:32:15.510
everyone thinks all
these Kubernetes

00:32:15.510 --> 00:32:16.410
workloads are ephemeral.

00:32:16.410 --> 00:32:17.327
They're generally not.

00:32:17.327 --> 00:32:19.160
There's a lot of stuff
that does need state.

00:32:19.160 --> 00:32:20.868
So there's lots of
good storage providers

00:32:20.868 --> 00:32:23.065
out there that we can leverage.

00:32:23.065 --> 00:32:25.440
Next, we're going to talk
about autoscaling a little bit.

00:32:25.440 --> 00:32:27.660
Autoscaling seems to be
a conversation that I

00:32:27.660 --> 00:32:29.080
have with every customer.

00:32:29.080 --> 00:32:31.358
And we always have lots
of religious debates

00:32:31.358 --> 00:32:32.400
on what they really want.

00:32:32.400 --> 00:32:35.850
But Optiva really has a
business case for this.

00:32:35.850 --> 00:32:37.360
And it's built into
their platform.

00:32:37.360 --> 00:32:39.090
So, Dan, do you want to talk
about just the advantages

00:32:39.090 --> 00:32:40.470
and why you guys feel
it's so important

00:32:40.470 --> 00:32:41.512
and what it does for you?

00:32:41.915 --> 00:32:42.540
DAN DYER: Sure.

00:32:42.540 --> 00:32:46.440
So in the telecom business,
especially calling,

00:32:46.440 --> 00:32:49.540
for example, or even data,
it tends to be very cyclical.

00:32:49.540 --> 00:32:53.520
So both across the day you
can see cycles, as well as

00:32:53.520 --> 00:32:54.210
across the year.

00:32:54.210 --> 00:32:56.720
So first thing in
the morning, you

00:32:56.720 --> 00:32:57.965
might have a spike in calls.

00:32:57.965 --> 00:33:00.340
Or on a certain holiday, you
might have a spike in calls.

00:33:00.340 --> 00:33:03.330
So if you look at this
chart that we have here,

00:33:03.330 --> 00:33:07.180
that's what that wavy curve is--
the sine wave kind of thing.

00:33:07.180 --> 00:33:12.750
And you also tend to have
some kind of long-term trend

00:33:12.750 --> 00:33:13.950
in terms of growth.

00:33:13.950 --> 00:33:15.815
Or you might even
have long-term trend--

00:33:15.815 --> 00:33:17.940
hopefully not, but you
might have a long-term trend

00:33:17.940 --> 00:33:19.900
in drop of traffic.

00:33:19.900 --> 00:33:23.610
And so if you look at
that step line there,

00:33:23.610 --> 00:33:25.080
that's traditionally
how you have

00:33:25.080 --> 00:33:29.100
dealt with it in a hardware,
bare metal kind of environment.

00:33:29.100 --> 00:33:31.150
You have to predict in advance.

00:33:31.150 --> 00:33:33.060
You have to over-provision.

00:33:33.060 --> 00:33:37.480
If you under-provision, then you
get that little red triangle,

00:33:37.480 --> 00:33:40.825
which means you've just failed
your application on a probably

00:33:40.825 --> 00:33:41.700
pretty regular basis.

00:33:41.700 --> 00:33:43.140
People are dropping calls.

00:33:43.140 --> 00:33:46.120
And if you're way down
there below the blue line,

00:33:46.120 --> 00:33:48.030
then you're
over-provisioned and you're

00:33:48.030 --> 00:33:50.280
paying for a bunch of
hardware to generate heat.

00:33:50.280 --> 00:33:52.733
So it's really critical
for our business.

00:33:52.733 --> 00:33:54.900
And there's a lot of other
businesses like this that

00:33:54.900 --> 00:33:56.790
have these kind of patterns.

00:33:56.790 --> 00:34:01.710
And so in the public cloud,
this is a no-brainer.

00:34:01.710 --> 00:34:04.570
You can provision VMs
in the background.

00:34:04.570 --> 00:34:07.955
But even in a private
cloud environment,

00:34:07.955 --> 00:34:09.330
if you have shared
infrastructure

00:34:09.330 --> 00:34:13.230
with a little bit of
additional capacity,

00:34:13.230 --> 00:34:15.060
or you're trying
to do some testing,

00:34:15.060 --> 00:34:19.469
or even you have, say, a
special holiday that you

00:34:19.469 --> 00:34:23.010
need to pre-provision
for, this scenario

00:34:23.010 --> 00:34:26.429
can take advantage of the
autoscaling capabilities

00:34:26.429 --> 00:34:30.389
that we have in GKE and
also in our application

00:34:30.389 --> 00:34:32.730
so that you're not
wasting a lot of hardware

00:34:32.730 --> 00:34:35.857
and you're able to
adapt as you need to go.

00:34:35.857 --> 00:34:37.440
KYLE BASSETT: So
we're going to show--

00:34:37.440 --> 00:34:39.929
when Dan and I first talked,
one of the big things

00:34:39.929 --> 00:34:41.909
we wanted-- success criteria--
was, let's get to the point

00:34:41.909 --> 00:34:42.867
where we can autoscale.

00:34:42.867 --> 00:34:45.300
So the discussion went,
well, pods are somewhat easy.

00:34:45.300 --> 00:34:48.600
Their application
can scale in cloud.

00:34:48.600 --> 00:34:51.210
Google's got tons of capacity,
so that part of the problem's

00:34:51.210 --> 00:34:53.290
pretty easy to solve.

00:34:53.290 --> 00:34:55.560
But in the VMware world,
we need to have capacity.

00:34:55.560 --> 00:34:57.750
And, essentially, another
node is another VM.

00:34:57.750 --> 00:34:59.965
So we can orchestrate
and automate that.

00:34:59.965 --> 00:35:01.590
Since the whole
platform's declarative,

00:35:01.590 --> 00:35:04.230
as we talked about,
it was pretty doable.

00:35:04.230 --> 00:35:06.600
We've done some work with
Configuration Manager

00:35:06.600 --> 00:35:09.270
to be able to do this
more in a GitOps fashion

00:35:09.270 --> 00:35:11.842
so that everything would
be source of truth in Git.

00:35:11.842 --> 00:35:13.800
You do a pull request,
you change a node count,

00:35:13.800 --> 00:35:16.300
gets approved, automatically
goes and spins up the clusters.

00:35:16.300 --> 00:35:18.090
So we'll show you the
capability of that.

00:35:18.090 --> 00:35:19.800
We've also-- we're
putting out a blog,

00:35:19.800 --> 00:35:22.900
I think later today,
that demos that.

00:35:22.900 --> 00:35:25.260
But you can see here, we
have 11 nodes running.

00:35:25.260 --> 00:35:27.630
And what this is,
this OCS process

00:35:27.630 --> 00:35:31.080
is essentially the workers that
are processing all the call

00:35:31.080 --> 00:35:33.810
volume and doing all the
analytics and all the billing.

00:35:33.810 --> 00:35:35.310
So we can only
handle so much load.

00:35:35.310 --> 00:35:37.860
And Optiva's
application, when it

00:35:37.860 --> 00:35:41.240
gets under certain conditions,
it knows how to scale up.

00:35:41.240 --> 00:35:42.990
So we're going to go
through the use case.

00:35:42.990 --> 00:35:46.703
And you can see here, we're
using Grafana and Prometheus

00:35:46.703 --> 00:35:47.370
on the back end.

00:35:47.370 --> 00:35:48.745
That's part of
their application.

00:35:48.745 --> 00:35:52.300
So the green represents
calls per second.

00:35:52.300 --> 00:35:55.050
So this test, we've got up to
about 60,000 calls per second

00:35:55.050 --> 00:35:56.340
with this environment.

00:35:56.340 --> 00:35:59.260
The blue line represents number
of pods that are running.

00:35:59.260 --> 00:36:01.740
So we're generally running
that one pod that I showed you.

00:36:01.740 --> 00:36:05.160
But once load comes up, we
need to scale to two and then

00:36:05.160 --> 00:36:07.472
beyond if we keep
cranking it up.

00:36:07.472 --> 00:36:08.930
So if you look at
these graphs, you

00:36:08.930 --> 00:36:13.980
can see we were upwards of
30,000 transactions here.

00:36:13.980 --> 00:36:16.570
We scaled down, so our
pod scaled back down.

00:36:16.570 --> 00:36:18.780
So this is pod-level
scaling in Kubernetes,

00:36:18.780 --> 00:36:20.670
working off metrics.

00:36:20.670 --> 00:36:25.357
We're pushing about 10,000 a
second at this point in time.

00:36:25.357 --> 00:36:26.940
So this stuff's
fairly out of the box.

00:36:26.940 --> 00:36:29.130
And you can see we
ran a lot of tests.

00:36:29.130 --> 00:36:31.380
And we were pushing load,
dropping load, pushing load,

00:36:31.380 --> 00:36:31.890
dropping load.

00:36:31.890 --> 00:36:33.473
So you can see, it's
very consistently

00:36:33.473 --> 00:36:34.970
scaling up and scaling down.

00:36:34.970 --> 00:36:36.690
And our error rates
are almost nothing.

00:36:36.690 --> 00:36:38.790
We're able to handle the load.

00:36:38.790 --> 00:36:41.160
Optiva's got a nice little
load generator for us.

00:36:41.160 --> 00:36:43.620
So they built a
great little tool

00:36:43.620 --> 00:36:46.410
that we can go in and
just slide the bar.

00:36:46.410 --> 00:36:50.340
So we'll jump it up to about
40,000 a second right now.

00:36:50.340 --> 00:36:51.780
The nice part is,
we're leveraging

00:36:51.780 --> 00:36:52.860
Prometheus on the back end.

00:36:52.860 --> 00:36:54.402
Everything's real
time on the graphs.

00:36:54.402 --> 00:36:57.040
So you can see, their
software has said,

00:36:57.040 --> 00:37:00.510
Kubernetes says, let's spin
up another pod with three more

00:37:00.510 --> 00:37:01.890
containers to handle this.

00:37:01.890 --> 00:37:06.180
So load goes up pretty quickly.

00:37:06.180 --> 00:37:10.380
Pod scales to two or starts to
bring all the containers up.

00:37:10.380 --> 00:37:13.770
And Dan mentioned that red
line in his diagram earlier.

00:37:13.770 --> 00:37:15.060
So we're up to 40,000.

00:37:15.060 --> 00:37:17.075
The red line's
representing, our error rate

00:37:17.075 --> 00:37:20.430
is starting to go up because,
as I showed you earlier,

00:37:20.430 --> 00:37:23.010
we are pushing the cluster,
from a resource perspective,

00:37:23.010 --> 00:37:28.032
pretty hard with our five
nodes and our 11 nodes

00:37:28.032 --> 00:37:28.740
on the back side.

00:37:28.740 --> 00:37:30.115
But here, you can
represent, now,

00:37:30.115 --> 00:37:31.620
the second pod is running here.

00:37:31.620 --> 00:37:32.760
DAN DYER: Just one comment.

00:37:32.760 --> 00:37:34.830
The scaling we're
doing here is--

00:37:34.830 --> 00:37:36.360
this is a nice
thing, once again,

00:37:36.360 --> 00:37:38.910
if you're not that
familiar with Kubernetes.

00:37:38.910 --> 00:37:41.815
This is just a standard
autoscaler based on CPU.

00:37:41.815 --> 00:37:43.440
You can do a lot more
elaborate things.

00:37:43.440 --> 00:37:44.815
And we're looking
at implementing

00:37:44.815 --> 00:37:45.990
those in the future.

00:37:45.990 --> 00:37:48.740
But getting something like
this working in Kubernetes

00:37:48.740 --> 00:37:52.080
and in this environment
is not rocket science.

00:37:52.080 --> 00:37:55.650
So if you structured
your application properly

00:37:55.650 --> 00:37:58.770
so that the pieces can be
independently run and scaled

00:37:58.770 --> 00:38:02.040
in containers, then it's pretty
straightforward to just build

00:38:02.040 --> 00:38:04.800
yourself an autoscaler in
there that can handle that

00:38:04.800 --> 00:38:07.240
based on CPU load, for example.

00:38:07.240 --> 00:38:09.490
KYLE BASSETT: So we've
scaled the load back down.

00:38:09.490 --> 00:38:11.323
So things are going to
start behaving again,

00:38:11.323 --> 00:38:14.430
because it's going to match
the capacity that we need.

00:38:14.430 --> 00:38:17.408
You'll see that Kubernetes is
terminating the other pods.

00:38:17.408 --> 00:38:18.450
We don't need it anymore.

00:38:18.450 --> 00:38:19.870
Now we're back down to one load.

00:38:19.870 --> 00:38:22.170
We're back down to a
standard processing.

00:38:22.170 --> 00:38:24.360
Now, obviously, the
issue with that use case

00:38:24.360 --> 00:38:25.920
was, we didn't have enough
nodes in the back end.

00:38:25.920 --> 00:38:28.380
So we have to solve the node
scaling problem so that we can

00:38:28.380 --> 00:38:30.810
solve the pod scaling problem.

00:38:30.810 --> 00:38:33.283
And everything in GKE, I
mentioned, is declarative.

00:38:33.283 --> 00:38:35.700
So Wes, do you want to comment
on just the approach of how

00:38:35.700 --> 00:38:38.310
you built the automation and why
you built it the way you did?

00:38:38.310 --> 00:38:39.227
WESTON HUTCHINS: Yeah.

00:38:39.227 --> 00:38:41.390
So a lot of the
architecture for GKE On-Prem

00:38:41.390 --> 00:38:44.205
is based off of something
in the open source

00:38:44.205 --> 00:38:46.080
we've been working on
called the cluster API.

00:38:46.080 --> 00:38:50.377
And what it allows you
to do is define the shape

00:38:50.377 --> 00:38:52.710
of your cluster, the Kubernetes
version, the add-ons you

00:38:52.710 --> 00:38:53.825
want to install.

00:38:53.825 --> 00:38:55.450
And then you can also
define the nodes.

00:38:55.450 --> 00:38:58.380
So you can define
what images you're

00:38:58.380 --> 00:39:01.020
going to deploy on them, what
Kubernetes components are

00:39:01.020 --> 00:39:01.980
running on those.

00:39:01.980 --> 00:39:03.630
And what Kyle's is
going to show here

00:39:03.630 --> 00:39:05.460
is, scaling, at this
point, is really

00:39:05.460 --> 00:39:07.440
just a simple matter
of going and changing

00:39:07.440 --> 00:39:10.800
the node count from 11
to 12, checking that

00:39:10.800 --> 00:39:12.750
into a Git repository.

00:39:12.750 --> 00:39:15.000
And then the cluster is
looking for these changes,

00:39:15.000 --> 00:39:16.017
syncing them down.

00:39:16.017 --> 00:39:18.600
And then it's just a matter of
our machinery going and saying,

00:39:18.600 --> 00:39:19.600
hey, I noticed a change.

00:39:19.600 --> 00:39:21.270
I need to move from 11 to 12.

00:39:21.270 --> 00:39:23.910
Let's go and bump
the cluster up.

00:39:23.910 --> 00:39:27.660
And the really nice
thing about this is--

00:39:27.660 --> 00:39:29.802
you'll probably hear the
term "cluster is cattle."

00:39:29.802 --> 00:39:32.010
We're going to start moving
down this direction where

00:39:32.010 --> 00:39:35.400
you can have these portable
metadata environments that

00:39:35.400 --> 00:39:36.420
describe your clusters.

00:39:36.420 --> 00:39:38.400
And you can spin these up in
different locations really

00:39:38.400 --> 00:39:38.900
quickly.

00:39:38.900 --> 00:39:41.510
But the source of truth
is all in a Git repo.

00:39:41.510 --> 00:39:42.600
And it's all declarative.

00:39:42.600 --> 00:39:44.340
And you can put
[INAUDIBLE] around that.

00:39:44.340 --> 00:39:46.380
You can put change request
validation and things

00:39:46.380 --> 00:39:47.070
like that.

00:39:47.070 --> 00:39:49.252
Super nice way to deploy
and manage clusters.

00:39:49.252 --> 00:39:51.210
KYLE BASSETT: So I paused
it here for a second.

00:39:51.210 --> 00:39:55.950
The bottom right-hand corner is
us monitoring the vCenter API.

00:39:55.950 --> 00:39:59.040
So you see, we saved the file
in the top left automatically.

00:39:59.040 --> 00:40:01.830
We've got vCenter API
picking up that that

00:40:01.830 --> 00:40:03.960
changed the declarative nature.

00:40:03.960 --> 00:40:06.150
And we'll flip over to
vCenter in a second.

00:40:06.150 --> 00:40:08.370
You're going to see that
the nodes are automatically

00:40:08.370 --> 00:40:09.030
coming up.

00:40:09.030 --> 00:40:11.380
And they're going to get
added to the cluster.

00:40:11.380 --> 00:40:14.070
So you see here, on the bottom,
we're powering on the VM

00:40:14.070 --> 00:40:15.660
from template.

00:40:15.660 --> 00:40:19.170
Within a very short period
of time, the node comes up.

00:40:19.170 --> 00:40:20.010
You can see it's up.

00:40:20.010 --> 00:40:21.750
We don't have an IP
address yet, but it's

00:40:21.750 --> 00:40:24.040
going to get an IP
address very quickly.

00:40:24.040 --> 00:40:25.890
And it's going to
join the cluster.

00:40:25.890 --> 00:40:28.330
And now we've got an
extra node in the cluster.

00:40:28.330 --> 00:40:31.020
So you see on the bottom left,
we've got our node online now.

00:40:31.020 --> 00:40:32.400
It's been on for 1 second.

00:40:32.400 --> 00:40:33.400
It's not ready.

00:40:33.400 --> 00:40:35.945
It's bootstrapping.

00:40:35.945 --> 00:40:37.320
And that'll change
pretty quickly

00:40:37.320 --> 00:40:39.762
and be able to come
onto a ready state.

00:40:39.762 --> 00:40:41.970
One of the interesting things
that we found out early

00:40:41.970 --> 00:40:48.340
is, Optiva in GKE makes a couple
kernel tweaks to their nodes.

00:40:48.340 --> 00:40:50.485
So we had never
really tested that.

00:40:50.485 --> 00:40:52.110
So it was good to
know that we actually

00:40:52.110 --> 00:40:53.880
could make those
tweaks that they

00:40:53.880 --> 00:40:55.260
needed, that they had in cloud.

00:40:55.260 --> 00:40:58.170
So top-right corner-- we
just solved this, currently,

00:40:58.170 --> 00:40:59.670
with an Ansible playbook.

00:40:59.670 --> 00:41:02.490
So we wrote an idempotent
Ansible playbook.

00:41:02.490 --> 00:41:05.280
We just add to the
inventory file, run it.

00:41:05.280 --> 00:41:08.010
It'll automatically make the
kernel tweak, reboot the host.

00:41:08.010 --> 00:41:11.160
And now the node's a first-class
citizen in the cluster.

00:41:11.160 --> 00:41:14.525
Plan on this is, since
we have a VM template,

00:41:14.525 --> 00:41:17.145
what we're going to do is
just put it into the template.

00:41:17.145 --> 00:41:19.270
And then we won't need the
Ansible playbook to run.

00:41:19.270 --> 00:41:21.030
So it'll just be spun
into the template.

00:41:21.030 --> 00:41:21.540
WESTON HUTCHINS:
I know this can be

00:41:21.540 --> 00:41:23.290
kind of hard to see
for folks in the back,

00:41:23.290 --> 00:41:24.600
but we'll publish this online.

00:41:24.600 --> 00:41:26.100
And we'll also have
a blog post that

00:41:26.100 --> 00:41:27.830
goes into a lot more
of these details.

00:41:27.830 --> 00:41:28.630
KYLE BASSETT: Yeah.

00:41:28.630 --> 00:41:31.350
So in this case, this is
just straight up Ansible

00:41:31.350 --> 00:41:33.150
We're running it
against the node.

00:41:33.150 --> 00:41:35.910
The node's going
to take the change.

00:41:35.910 --> 00:41:38.640
You can see it skipped all
the ones that we've already

00:41:38.640 --> 00:41:39.945
made the change to.

00:41:39.945 --> 00:41:41.860
The node will go not ready.

00:41:41.860 --> 00:41:43.260
And then it's really fast.

00:41:43.260 --> 00:41:47.050
Within 20, 30 seconds,
the node comes back up.

00:41:47.050 --> 00:41:49.930
And what you'll find is,
when I describe the node,

00:41:49.930 --> 00:41:52.020
we've automatically deployed
all the daemon sets.

00:41:52.020 --> 00:41:53.850
So we have a Sysdig daemon set.

00:41:53.850 --> 00:41:55.950
We've got a bunch of
different daemon sets

00:41:55.950 --> 00:41:59.400
that are running for the
environment that we need.

00:41:59.400 --> 00:42:01.650
And now, we'll get to
the point where, let's

00:42:01.650 --> 00:42:02.970
scale this up even higher.

00:42:02.970 --> 00:42:05.730
And let's see how it performs
this time, from an [? air ?]

00:42:05.730 --> 00:42:06.430
perspective.

00:42:06.430 --> 00:42:10.608
So we pump it up to 62,000
transactions per second.

00:42:10.608 --> 00:42:12.900
You're going to immediately
see, the Grafana dashboards

00:42:12.900 --> 00:42:13.830
are going to ramp up.

00:42:13.830 --> 00:42:16.830
We're going to get
our second pod scaled.

00:42:16.830 --> 00:42:18.240
And we don't have
a lot of errors

00:42:18.240 --> 00:42:19.990
because we've got the
right capacity to be

00:42:19.990 --> 00:42:22.800
able to handle the volume.

00:42:22.800 --> 00:42:25.190
So having these load
generators and loads tests

00:42:25.190 --> 00:42:27.690
were just really critical for
us to be able to actually test

00:42:27.690 --> 00:42:28.403
this stuff.

00:42:28.403 --> 00:42:30.570
Trial and error, some
different tweaks and settings.

00:42:30.570 --> 00:42:32.730
And having a really
good monitoring setup

00:42:32.730 --> 00:42:36.060
allowed us to get
the baseline of that.

00:42:36.060 --> 00:42:38.355
So two other quick
things to show you.

00:42:38.355 --> 00:42:40.230
Everyone's wondering,
why haven't they talked

00:42:40.230 --> 00:42:41.380
about the networking yet?

00:42:41.380 --> 00:42:43.640
So we'll talk about
networking quickly.

00:42:43.640 --> 00:42:46.140
Wes, do you want to comment
just generally on the networking

00:42:46.140 --> 00:42:47.200
strategy and [INAUDIBLE]?

00:42:47.200 --> 00:42:48.660
WESTON HUTCHINS: Yeah.

00:42:48.660 --> 00:42:52.340
So the way we've architected
the footprint of GKE On-Prem

00:42:52.340 --> 00:42:53.940
is that each cluster
is an island.

00:42:53.940 --> 00:42:56.730
So they form an IP
mesh in between them.

00:42:56.730 --> 00:42:59.250
But everything that is
connected outside the cluster

00:42:59.250 --> 00:43:00.840
has to get [? natted ?] out.

00:43:00.840 --> 00:43:04.232
We are using BGP, so we do
have a theoretical 50-node max

00:43:04.232 --> 00:43:06.690
for a lot of these cluster
sizes that we've started out at.

00:43:06.690 --> 00:43:09.390
But we're going to
expand this over time.

00:43:09.390 --> 00:43:11.790
And we use Calico
for network policy.

00:43:11.790 --> 00:43:13.290
I already talked about F5.

00:43:13.290 --> 00:43:14.930
We use that for the
L4 load balancer

00:43:14.930 --> 00:43:17.180
where we can actually go and
spin up service-type load

00:43:17.180 --> 00:43:20.173
balancer and have that
route traffic into it.

00:43:20.173 --> 00:43:21.840
And eventually, we
will have the ability

00:43:21.840 --> 00:43:25.700
to make that plugable so you
can actually go and bring

00:43:25.700 --> 00:43:28.778
a different load balancer
service in there.

00:43:28.778 --> 00:43:31.320
KYLE BASSETT: So I mentioned
how hard it is to carve storage.

00:43:31.320 --> 00:43:32.760
We all know how
hard it is to get

00:43:32.760 --> 00:43:35.040
a rule put into your
F5 box, and how many

00:43:35.040 --> 00:43:36.858
tickets and coffees that takes.

00:43:36.858 --> 00:43:38.900
This is, if you're not
familiar with Kubernetes--

00:43:38.900 --> 00:43:40.440
it's kind of the same thing.

00:43:40.440 --> 00:43:43.140
We just run a simple
kubectl command.

00:43:43.140 --> 00:43:45.270
We've got a famous
beer Giphy app

00:43:45.270 --> 00:43:46.697
that we've just deployed there.

00:43:46.697 --> 00:43:48.030
We've also created the endpoint.

00:43:48.030 --> 00:43:50.910
This is the entire
configuration of the endpoint.

00:43:50.910 --> 00:43:52.480
This integrates into F5.

00:43:52.480 --> 00:43:54.550
So all of this is
sending API calls.

00:43:54.550 --> 00:43:56.240
You can see, we
go back to our F5.

00:43:56.240 --> 00:43:57.810
We're in the user space.

00:43:57.810 --> 00:44:00.270
We've got four endpoints.

00:44:00.270 --> 00:44:05.543
If I refresh, we automatically
get our default ingress.

00:44:05.543 --> 00:44:06.960
If we go look at
that, you can see

00:44:06.960 --> 00:44:09.570
we've got a cluster
IP we're leveraging.

00:44:09.570 --> 00:44:12.150
If we go to our application
that we just deployed,

00:44:12.150 --> 00:44:15.120
we've got our random
beer Giphy up and running

00:44:15.120 --> 00:44:17.610
in a matter of seconds.

00:44:17.610 --> 00:44:20.100
So that's it for the demo side.

00:44:20.100 --> 00:44:23.400
Maybe we'll flip back to the
slides and just close out.

00:44:23.400 --> 00:44:26.200
So tips and lessons learned--
everyone's probably interested

00:44:26.200 --> 00:44:26.700
on that.

00:44:26.700 --> 00:44:28.770
I think we can all
make a few comments.

00:44:28.770 --> 00:44:30.840
Environment sizing is important.

00:44:30.840 --> 00:44:32.370
We knew this was
a big application.

00:44:32.370 --> 00:44:33.810
When we first started
with four nodes,

00:44:33.810 --> 00:44:35.185
we got the
application up, but we

00:44:35.185 --> 00:44:38.550
were right at the edge of
leveraging the amount of CPU

00:44:38.550 --> 00:44:39.730
and memory that we needed.

00:44:39.730 --> 00:44:43.330
So Optiva, being a very
agile team-- this cluster

00:44:43.330 --> 00:44:45.540
is actually deployed in Berlin.

00:44:45.540 --> 00:44:47.522
We were able to get
another node added to it

00:44:47.522 --> 00:44:49.230
and were able to keep
the project moving.

00:44:49.230 --> 00:44:51.780
So make sure you size
your environment.

00:44:51.780 --> 00:44:54.150
We got into a few
interesting issues

00:44:54.150 --> 00:44:56.100
that we need to look into.

00:44:56.100 --> 00:44:59.130
When we were pushing
load heavy against pods

00:44:59.130 --> 00:45:01.745
that had PV
requirements, DRS was

00:45:01.745 --> 00:45:03.870
starting to do more than
it should have and started

00:45:03.870 --> 00:45:05.090
moving environments around.

00:45:05.090 --> 00:45:08.530
So we decided to disable DRS
for a lot of these environments.

00:45:08.530 --> 00:45:12.150
We really didn't need
stuff moving around.

00:45:12.150 --> 00:45:15.870
I mentioned monitoring
tools, getting all that stuff

00:45:15.870 --> 00:45:19.410
in place early so you
have a good baseline.

00:45:19.410 --> 00:45:21.310
Anything else you guys
want to add to the--

00:45:21.310 --> 00:45:22.110
DAN DYER: Yeah.

00:45:22.110 --> 00:45:24.780
One of the things I think
is a natural tendency

00:45:24.780 --> 00:45:28.290
is to spend a lot
of time thinking

00:45:28.290 --> 00:45:31.020
about your operational
model before you do this.

00:45:31.020 --> 00:45:33.540
A lot of people try to try
it out and see what happens.

00:45:33.540 --> 00:45:37.190
And I think that it's really
critical, especially jumping

00:45:37.190 --> 00:45:39.420
into new environment like
this, to really think

00:45:39.420 --> 00:45:41.910
about your operational
model and things

00:45:41.910 --> 00:45:44.160
like what type of monitoring
you need to put in place.

00:45:44.160 --> 00:45:46.010
What do you want to monitor?

00:45:46.010 --> 00:45:48.660
Who's getting access
to that kind of stuff?

00:45:48.660 --> 00:45:52.410
I think the other really
cool thing about this was--

00:45:52.410 --> 00:45:55.530
and we already alluded to
this, but we had already

00:45:55.530 --> 00:45:59.850
spent the time to move our
app to GKE in the cloud.

00:45:59.850 --> 00:46:03.500
But I was expecting
a lot more work

00:46:03.500 --> 00:46:05.935
in terms of refactoring
our scripts,

00:46:05.935 --> 00:46:07.310
in terms of
deployment and things

00:46:07.310 --> 00:46:09.110
like that, to get this to work.

00:46:09.110 --> 00:46:11.580
And yet, we had a pretty
minimal amount of work there.

00:46:11.580 --> 00:46:13.043
So that's a really nice thing.

00:46:13.043 --> 00:46:14.960
So if you're going to
invest the time in here,

00:46:14.960 --> 00:46:19.220
I think the promise of GKE
On-Prem relative to the cloud--

00:46:19.220 --> 00:46:21.495
it's definitely
delivering on that.

00:46:21.495 --> 00:46:23.370
KYLE BASSETT: The other
piece I would mention

00:46:23.370 --> 00:46:28.670
is, put some time into your
CICD deployment strategies.

00:46:28.670 --> 00:46:30.102
Optiva had done a
really good job.

00:46:30.102 --> 00:46:31.560
So there was a
couple of times when

00:46:31.560 --> 00:46:33.780
we tested some weird things
and we wanted to start

00:46:33.780 --> 00:46:35.370
from a clean slate again.

00:46:35.370 --> 00:46:37.770
And that was an hour
and a half effort

00:46:37.770 --> 00:46:40.740
to go and redeploy their entire
application after we built

00:46:40.740 --> 00:46:43.860
the new cluster, which
was maybe a 40, 50 minute

00:46:43.860 --> 00:46:46.450
exercise because we've
automated a lot of it.

00:46:46.450 --> 00:46:48.120
So it gave us a
clean slate again.

00:46:48.120 --> 00:46:50.195
And if we saw weird
things happening

00:46:50.195 --> 00:46:51.570
because we were
experimenting, we

00:46:51.570 --> 00:46:53.010
didn't have to
troubleshoot and wonder

00:46:53.010 --> 00:46:54.150
if that was causing something.

00:46:54.150 --> 00:46:55.608
We just repaved
the infrastructure.

00:46:55.608 --> 00:46:58.350
Let's just rebuild it and let's
start from a clean slate again.

00:46:58.350 --> 00:47:01.770
So this cluster got built
Sunday night, again.

00:47:01.770 --> 00:47:03.360
And then we did this
demo last night.

00:47:03.360 --> 00:47:06.000
So we're really comfortable at
standing it up and rebuilding

00:47:06.000 --> 00:47:07.020
it again.

00:47:07.020 --> 00:47:09.240
Other things to think about is--

00:47:09.240 --> 00:47:11.850
Dan mentioned it, but
leveraging Helm charts

00:47:11.850 --> 00:47:14.400
and just spending an
extra couple hours

00:47:14.400 --> 00:47:16.920
writing that Ansible playbook
saved us a lot of time

00:47:16.920 --> 00:47:17.910
down the road.

00:47:17.910 --> 00:47:19.410
So when we had to
build the cluster,

00:47:19.410 --> 00:47:21.780
we didn't have to
SSH into 11 VMs

00:47:21.780 --> 00:47:23.490
and go and do the
kernel parameter.

00:47:23.490 --> 00:47:26.730
So just roll up your sleeves,
do some Google searching.

00:47:26.730 --> 00:47:28.230
Most of the
information's out there.

00:47:28.230 --> 00:47:29.025
Reach out to us.

00:47:29.025 --> 00:47:30.900
We'll share some of
these playbooks and stuff

00:47:30.900 --> 00:47:33.510
that we've figured
out along the way.

00:47:33.510 --> 00:47:35.670
And the only other
thing I would say

00:47:35.670 --> 00:47:38.280
is, we did a stand up
every day with the team.

00:47:38.280 --> 00:47:39.750
And everybody showed up to it.

00:47:39.750 --> 00:47:41.160
Some days, it was just a chat.

00:47:41.160 --> 00:47:43.275
But it also removed
any roadblocks

00:47:43.275 --> 00:47:45.900
and kept everybody really in the
loop [? which ?] was going on.

00:47:45.900 --> 00:47:47.543
So being a distributed
team and not

00:47:47.543 --> 00:47:48.960
seeing each other
all the time, we

00:47:48.960 --> 00:47:51.160
had 11 o'clock
stand up every day

00:47:51.160 --> 00:47:53.620
and it really helped the
project move forward.

00:47:53.620 --> 00:47:56.105
And it helped us get
rid of any roadblocks

00:47:56.105 --> 00:47:58.230
and also provided good
updates to the stakeholders,

00:47:58.230 --> 00:47:59.730
because we had a
pretty short runway

00:47:59.730 --> 00:48:01.273
to get all this ready for Next.

00:48:01.273 --> 00:48:02.190
WESTON HUTCHINS: Yeah.

00:48:02.190 --> 00:48:03.930
I would say-- and
there's probably

00:48:03.930 --> 00:48:07.150
going to be a lot of questions--
we'll be at the booth a lot.

00:48:07.150 --> 00:48:09.600
You can also find us
on Twitter, email us.

00:48:09.600 --> 00:48:11.100
We'll figure out
how to get in touch

00:48:11.100 --> 00:48:13.010
and answer those
questions for you.

00:48:13.010 --> 00:48:14.760
And I guess the
closing thing is just,

00:48:14.760 --> 00:48:18.440
if you guys liked this talk,
go and give us some feedback.

00:48:18.440 --> 00:48:20.390
And we'll be around.

